+## 2026-10-23T18:09:31.0074193+08:00 (UTC+08:00) - Drain the original player item buffer on native quit
+
+- Provider .5 successfully drained final SQL queues but the identical native stop audit still lost its original three named snowballs: the per-player item buffer normally transfers on a later player tick. The failed .5 world, source, artifact and log remain preserved in the consuming project.
+- Provider .6 exposes the mixin's original buffer-to-repository operation as griefLogger$flushItemQueue, delegates the tick to it, and invokes it on native PLAYER_QUIT before recording QUIT. SERVER_STOPPED then drains the final SQL queues using .5's blocking ownership. No replacement observation, manual audit flush, guessed actor or synthetic item grant repairs the history.
+- Fresh Java 21 :neoforge:build succeeds. Actual-provider SQLite suite passes 109 assertions. Native final-after-last-tick shutdown passes eleven assertions and a separate clean JVM restart/second stop passes eight: all five block, one container and one item records persist once, original storage/components remain equal, and JOIN/QUIT advance once per session. Broader reader/writer/FULL/commerce regression evidence is recorded by the parent project when complete.
+- SQL queues/database stay at mod lifetime; this does not establish integrated physical-client lifecycle, failed-final-drain durability, unflushed JVM death or device loss. No production activation or deferred client changes. Author and committer use A-BenLi06 <ben_li06@outlook.com>.
